          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Figurative fornication or whoredom signifies spiritual and covenant unfaithfulness to God, especially idolatry and apostasy, and can include illicit dependence on foreign political powers. Literal sexual sin supplies the marital metaphor.\",\"book_summary\":\"Fornication or whoredom signifies covenant unfaithfulness through union with other gods or kingdoms; physical sexual sin provides the image.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Covenant unfaithfulness to God, chiefly idolatry or apostasy.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Covenant unfaithfulness by joining other gods or kingdoms outside the divinely established union.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement. The book explicitly includes both principal consensus cases: devotion to other gods and faithless alliance with other kingdoms. It is more concise and does not enumerate broader applications such as worldliness or economic corruption, but it does not clearly reject them.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book preserves the consensus identification and the physical-to-spiritual metaphor underlying it.\",\"rationale\":\"Both readings identify the symbol with breach of covenantal union with God. The book’s reference to other gods corresponds to the consensus emphasis on idolatry and apostasy, while its reference to other kingdoms corresponds to the recognized political-alliance application. Its statement that physical sin supplies the image also matches the consensus explanation of the marital metaphor.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The book may sound somewhat narrower than the broadest consensus formulations because it defines the breach specifically as joining other gods or kingdoms rather than mentioning every form of apostasy, worldliness, or corruption. However, those broader applications are secondary boundaries, not a different core identification.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
